  "slug": "adna-alignment-sensitivity-dbd7",
  "summary": "New aligners, assemblers, k-mer counters, accelerators and storage formats each target a distinct cost in processing large-scale sequencing data.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is reducing a specific computational cost in read processing, through algorithmic redesign or algorithm–hardware co-design. On alignment, minibwa combines BWA-MEM seeding with minimap2 chaining and is reported ~4x faster than BWA-MEM at comparable accuracy. lWFA/lBiWFA extend wavefront alignment to exact local alignment in O(sN) time. VEGA and Motivo move DP alignment and sequence-to-graph mapping onto RISC-V vector and ASIC designs. Assembly work includes Gallifrey, which uses locally consistent de Bruijn graphs and is reported >6x faster than rust-mdbg with half the memory, and HARICOT, which assembles raw nanopore signal without basecalling via fuzzy de Bruijn graphs. Tuna revisits sort-versus-hash k-mer counting with a cost model and reports outperforming KMC3. Storage is addressed by pbzarr for cohort-scale per-base data and by LRCBench, which compares 35 long-read compressors.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-opus-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-25"
}
